ears ago  where you know you use citrix to connect  to some like ms office applications or  you know sap or like lotus notes or  something uh off somewhere else i  imagine it's changed a bit since then um  is that still a common pattern or like  how are they you know  i  i imagine a lot has changed anyway  a lot has definitely changed all the way  up and down the stack  but if you consider the the fundamentals  of the virtualization solution to be  remote remotely connecting to an  application running on a different host  the fundamentals of the technology are  still the same uh they're still used to  solve a lot of the same problems but the  problem set that it's solving now has  actually gotten dramatically bigger and  we could tell a little bit about that as  we work our way through here  uh but the the citrix of today i mean  it's been around for  quite some time it's it's been solving  problems for enterprise i.t customers  around secure access application  delivery and even networking  technologies believe it or not  for well over 25 years  but back in the day say in the 20 25  years ago  the technology stack called wind frame  or metaframe at the time  was pitched as a remote access solution  and you know it it took the the model  if we look at corporate i.t back in that  day  it was typically enterprise would build  a data center it was their own building  they built their entire stack from  cooling and cabling all the way up  through cpu network uh to a secure  perimeter they build their own  applications that they'd run their app  stack inside of the data center they'd  install the app on their client  computers who were in a lot of cases in  that same office  clients would connect to their apps  client server was a thing  and uh and they were happy  well that use case started to fall down  as the complexity of it and users  expectations started to change  so as organizations started to to bring  in multiple data centers uh multiple  remote offices mergers and acquisitions  offshoring of developers you know  a very very broad range of different  influences started to come in  and that model where you run client-side  xc  uh on a client device  and server-side you know connecting to  the server side we'd had really chatty  protocols and stuff that we would  develop to back then  that model would break down so people  would leverage  session remoting as we'd consider it  today and  desktop and app virtualization  has a way to deliver applications to  users on whatever device they happen to  be via corporate owned or byo  uh on over whatever type of a network  the user happened to be on  that was kind of the foundation and that  foundation of the solution is still in  play still solving problems for  customers  uh today  we do however call it something a little  bit different today today we'd call it  uh vdi is the a popular term that we  would call that kind of uh solution  pattern and that's short for virtual  desktop infrastructure gotcha so virtual  desktop infrastructure what  what does that look like concretely for  folks today it you know are they still  running it all on their own data centers  what does it look like today so some  customers are still running it in their  own data centers and we call it vdi  today oddly enough  but  it's it's a bit different  so you'll hear the term das or desktop  as a service used today and while the  fundamentals of the solution are the  same  it's essentially a solution spectrum at  one end of the solution you've got all  customer managed infrastructure software  components control playing everything  typically on on stuff that they've  bought  in a perpetual manner and then built up  on stuff that they own as well  the other end of the spectrum with  desktop as a service similar to  infrastructure as a service database you  know all the as a services  that modern networking and cloud  technologies have brought us  um so you've got a bit of a continuum  so what we uh there are customers that  still run what we call daz today with a  citrix  control plan that they build and manage  their own in their own data center  but then at the other end of the  spectrum you've got the customers that  have evolved or maybe even started  in the cloud right the whole born in the  cloud type of generation  and so you know you've got it while  you've got a continuum in between there  uh at the far end of the spectrum and  the daz end of the spectrum  instead of the customer buying building  managing and maintaining the entire  stack top to bottom  they consume managed services or cloud  services from providers such as google  and  admittedly also citrix  and so in doing so they can focus on  the core business stuff that adds value  to their business and and offload these  specialized technology functions to the  experts  and then we we end up being responsible  for managing and maintaining and  updating and securing and providing high  availability and and all those kinds of  things  so it's a it's it's a bit of a different  world still the core of the technology  stack is the same  got it and  if i'm if i'm hearing this right it  sounds like another instance of this  pattern people sometimes called move and  improve you know where you take an  existing app you you kind of move it  over into cloud and then you start  iteratively adding kind of more managed  bits to it or more integrations to other  things  to make it work better  so for folks who are  currently running citrix in their own  data centers  what what does this move and improve  path look like concretely you know for  them  so you've got you've got essentially two  different components i'm simplifying  things a little bit but if you want to  call the  the two customer managed components in  the vdi model  you could call the control plane the  virtualization control plane that's what  i was talking about where they get the  software from citrix they download it  they install it on their own 